 {:seon.task/name "a-transient-model-failure-parks"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #28-29, re-read at 26363fe. (1) A provider 429 is answered to the agent: `seon.fx/worker-proc` hands the owner {:dest :outcome :budget-key :est :cost} without the request, and `seon.fx/owner-proc`'s :done replies the outcome to its asker at once (it also records the :effect problem and `seon.fx/settle` halves the bucket's rate, both right); `seon.turn/work!` then ends the attempt :error on that error map and the judge attaches a refinement chain, so the task's budget is spent on the provider saying \"wait\". Correct: a :seon.model/rate-limited outcome is the provider's retry-after, the one delayed insert AGENTS.md section 2 allows: the worker hands the owner the request it performed as :request; on a rate-limited outcome the owner does not answer the asker, records the problem as now, empties the bucket (the provider's own bucket is empty: tokens to 0, then AIMD halves the rate as now), and puts the request back on its queue under its old key, so `seon.fx/dispatch` waits on `seon.fx/refill` and `seon.fx/wake-later`'s tick sends it again; the asker (the agent's fx! in its turn) stays parked on its one answer, which is the call that went through or a non-transient error. (2) REFUTED, dropped from this task: the plan said the call is bounded by a clock (openai-compat timeout-ms 120000, connect 10000); 8fd2849 removed both, and `seon.model/http-post` now has no deadline (the reply ends when the server ends it), so the streamed-reply proof it asked for has no clock left to replace. Streaming as progress shown in the view, if wanted, is its own task. A transport failure (`seon.model/http-post`'s caught exception, no status) is not transient by any provider's statement: it still answers the agent and ends the attempt :error, recorded; re-sending it would be a blind retry. Estimate: per 429 one queue insert and one tick; no new model calls beyond the ones the provider refused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-admit-test/a-rate-limited-model-call-waits-for-the-bucket-and-is-answered}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-test-bound-derived-and-named"
  :seon.task/plan "Laws audit #1 (notes/AUDIT-2026-10-21-laws.md) and audit FENCES #22. A test run is killed by a clock: the literal 30000 ms sits at 8 sites, `seon.agent/handle!` (:tests, as the default), `seon.turn/observe`, `seon.reconcile/advance` and `seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc` (both :tested requests), `seon.root/run-tests!` (its 2-arity), `seon.root/merge!` (:merge-tests) and `seon.web/requests` (run-tests! and merge!), and `seon.root/ext-op` forwards :timeout-ms to the agent. `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` reads the child's pipe in 1 s slices against a deadline; past it the child is killed and the run is one :error report with no test var, so every proof reads :none and the attempt is refused: a task whose proofs take longer (the next-generation proof measured 74 s, 238db30) is judged red by time, not by its tests, and the agent is told only that nothing ran. v1 bounded each test by a declared per-test bound (default 5 s, seon.test/bounded-result in v1, ~/src/seon); here the law is AGENTS.md section 2, no clock decides, so the bound is deleted, not declared (this replaces the plan's earlier config key or derived bound). Correct: (1) no deadline anywhere: every request is {:op :tests :id a} or {:op :merge-tests ...} with no :timeout-ms, `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` takes no argument and blocks on the pipe ((rd -1)) to EOF, keeping only the last complete line, then reaps the child (`ffi-status` without kill); (2) the child writes (pr-str {:seon.test/started var}) as each test var begins (clojure.test's :begin-test-var report, which Jolt emits) and its result as the last line; (3) `seon.agent/run-answer` (interface written, stub body) turns that last line and the exit status into the run: the result when the line is one, else one :error report on the started test var with :exit status, so `seon.proofs/results` reads that test :error and the context shows it as a red proof with the death beside it. A run that hangs is ended only by an explicit kill of the agent (root's function; a stall is task `seon.fences/a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound`), and its death then names the test. Deletes the 8 literals, the :timeout-ms forwarding in `seon.root/ext-op`, the 3-arity of `seon.root/run-tests!`, the deadline loop and the :timeout branch of `run-tests-forked`; the example string \"tests timed out after 30000 ms; killed\" in seon.context-test's run-errors row becomes another whole-run error (a namespace that did not load). Estimate: no new work; the parent reads the same lines and parses one. PREMISES CHANGED (session TESTBOUND, at f67052e): 0d0e3fd had already deleted the 30000 ms literals and the :timeout-ms forwarding, replacing the deadline with a work budget (`seon.agent/run-tests-here` runs within :seon.config.agent/test-ticks engine ticks; owner: runaway work is bounded by the work it does, never a clock), named in docs/failure-points.md and asserted by bin/check robust (a test that never ends is stopped with :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it). The ticks are not a clock, so they stay: deleting them (the design step's implement plan) would make robust's endless test hang. What this task added: `seon.agent/run-answer` and the child's {:seon.test/started var} line, which name the test a death interrupted; `seon.root/fs-call!` lost its 30000 ms deref fallback."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-killed-test-run-names-the-test-that-was-running
                      seon.web-test/calls-become-requests}}
