 {:seon.task/name "leases-and-store-size-are-declared-bounds"
  :seon.task/plan "Part (1) replaced 2026-10-23 by store-wide ids (task seon.db/each-id-is-issued-once): no agent holds a block, so none can mint past one. Audit FENCES #17, #51. (1) Each agent mints entity ids from an AtomicLong set to its lease (seon.db/after-fork!, db.clj:117-122; lease = base + n*EID-LEASE, root.clj:173) and nothing stops it at lease + EID-LEASE (100,000): an agent minting more silently takes the next agent's ids, and two branches merge different entities under one id. (2) Real bounds are read outside seon.config: SEON_LMDB_MB 1024 (db.clj:60), SEON_TRIP_MB 4 and SEON_AGENT_HEAP_MB 1024 (agent.clj:25,29); with runs kept and one store of many branches the LMDB map fills, the writer refuses (named failure point) and nothing showed it coming. Correct: minting past the lease answers a typed :seon.db/lease-spent (an :err item in the agent's eval) or takes a fresh block through the writer, and the agent's stats show ids used of its lease; the three config keys live in seon.config with reasons; (c/state) and the dashboard show the map's used and allotted bytes; the writer's refusal names the map size. Estimate: one comparison per minted id. Session STORES' plan (2026-10-21, a Datahike change, waits for root's approval): the check belongs where the id is minted, beside the one that exists. reference-code/datahike src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc:58 next-eid already raises :entity-id/range when an id would reach tx0; the store's allocator (datahike.writing/eid-allocators, writing.cljc:841) gains a per-store ceiling for this process (an AtomicLong pair, or a ceilings map beside it), set by seon.db/after-fork! to lease + EID-LEASE, and next-eid raises {:error :entity-id/lease-spent :entity-id eid :ceiling c} past it, so the transaction is refused before anything applies (dw/apply! runs ahead, so a check after it would be too late). seon.agent/transact! reads the raise as :seon.db/lease-spent (an :err item naming the lease, its use and the fix). Root's own 