 {:seon.task/name "cost-tolerances-are-derived"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit FENCES #38, #40-42, #44. seon.cost/slack {:ns 3.0 :bytes 1.25 :calls 1.0} (cost.clj:40-43): bytes are called exact yet get 25% because the unit table flakes (seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache: 11.6 vs 8.9 KB); ns gets 3x for load. steeper-by 0.3 (cost.clj:45-49) and ratchet 1.5 (cost.clj:55-59) are guesses too. seon.context's prompt hardcodes 1.25x/1.0x/3x (context.clj:27), a second copy. test/seon/profile_test.clj:37-41 asserts within 2x and is gated (when contract/on?), zero assertions when off. Correct: each unit row records its spread over k runs under a declared cache state; a dimension's slack is the spread of the units its estimate prices (bytes then near 1.0); ns carries the run's load (uptime, the bench lock) and its spread at that load; the exponent tolerance is derived per dimension and proof as log(slack_d)/log(n_max/n_min) (over 100x: bytes 1.25 -> 0.048, ns 3 -> 0.24); the ratchet is the overrun's own measured spread at filing; every verdict prints the derived tolerances, and the prompt renders them from seon.cost. profile_test asserts the sampling rule exactly (ceil(n/64) timed calls) in an environment with contracts on. A first implementation is jolt/unitflake (2838e62, e7aad9d, c366a41, cc43b5b), re-targeted to main by session HARVEST (2026-10-22) as notes/worktrees/unitflake-retargeted-2026-10-22.diff (the bench in seon.cost-test since wave 1, the tolerance line in seon.render.ui/verdict-lines, docs/cost.md; applies on main 5af7b4b, src +53). Not merged, because applied on main it turns three green cost proofs red, so the derivation is wrong, not the code it judges: seon.docs-cost-test/an-unchanged-reload-costs-a-parse-and-a-pull-per-section and seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass go STEEPER on calls (n^1.01 and n^1.03 against a calls exponent tolerance of 0.000, derived because every unit row's calls range is exactly 1.0; a fixed per-call overhead bends a linear fit, and the old steeper-by 0.3 absorbed it), and seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program goes REGRESSION past its open overrun (the ratchet tightens from 1.5x to the bytes slack 1.26x). Also its unit table (prices and ranges of 2026-10-21) must be re-measured on main: :pr-str/char now measures 33.05 B against the row's 29.36 [0.999 1.023]. The plan owes: an exponent tolerance that holds for exact dimensions (from the fit's own residual, or the estimate's fixed term), the ratchet's rule stated, then the table re-measured with seon.cost-test/table."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span
                      seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread
                      seon.context-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances}}
